\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Product symmetry and the scope of optimizer conclusions}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{source-discrepancy}
\tag{transfer-limit}

\p{The relevant optimizer results appear in version 1 of
\citelink{https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.05136v1}, submitted 5 August 2026:
Sections 3--4 and 6, Appendices B.1--B.6 and B.9, with limitations in
Section 11.}

\p{The full product symmetry acts
on the factors by}

##{
 (U,V)\longmapsto(UA,VA^{-\mathsf T}).
}

\p{It does not use #{(UA,VA)}. For row-vector attention weights, the
corresponding action uses right multiplication, as in \ref{ftip-00BX}.}

\p{The general rectangular preconditioner representation need not have the
square-case uniqueness. A probability-one full-rank claim additionally needs
a law for the gradient; a generic full-rank observation does not specify one.}

\p{The coordinatewise theorem is a
zero-state, memoryless obstruction. The clock theorem is continuous-time and
excludes scalar-Adam's momentum. Experimental optimizer and attention results
remain observations reported by the source. Equivariance is not identified
with low-rank recovery.}
